@zinzied , 
I would like to work on this issue. 

My approach would be to add a periodic session snapshot mechanism that saves the active session state to a crash-recovery file at a configurable interval, and on next launch detects unclean shutdowns and offers to restore. Let me know if that sounds good or if there are any constraints I should be aware of.

It seems to me that because of the official opencode docs everyone is trying to use the OpenAI compatible API with ollama.
Unfortunately the Ollama OpenAI compatible api does not support setting context via a parameter like `num_ctx` that is available on the official Ollama API.
So if you use opencode with the openai compatible api and don't want to set a global context size value that is not model specific or don't want to wrap every model with a modelfile to set proper context size for each model, you will get the default ollama value that for most people will be 4096 tokens.

I personally use [ollama-ai-provider-v2](https://github.com/opencomp-eu/ollama-ai-provider-v2) currently:
```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",

  "provider": {
    "ollama": {
      "models": {
        "gemma4:12b-it-qat": {
          "id": "gemma4:12b-it-qat",
          "limit": {
            "context": 194560,
            "output": 32768
          },
          "name": "gemma4:12b-it-qat",
          "options": {
            "options": {
              "keep_alive": "10m",
              "num_ctx": 194560
            },
            "think": true
          },
          "tool_call": true
        }
      },
      "name": "Ollama",
      "npm": "ollama-ai-provider-v2",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://localhost:11434/api"
      }
    }
  }
}
```
because it lets me set the model context size, without this feature local models are useless in opencode.
I did not test this idea but I assume the default value for cloud ollama models is full context and not just measly 4096 so that's probably why @jclsn experienced the difference between them.

While [ollama-ai-provider-v2](https://github.com/opencomp-eu/ollama-ai-provider-v2) is kind of working, I noticed some quirks so it might not be the best solution and I assume that https://github.com/jagreehal/ai-sdk-ollama would probably be better, but I can't use it because I was not able to figure out how to make it send `num_ctx` by configuring it through opencode. If anyone figures out how to, please post the config here too.

@LifetimeVip @Hona I fixed the issue with PR #33116.

The `SessionTodoDock` rendered its checkbox list with Solid's `<Index>`, which only re-renders when the array length or item references change. The global todo store updates via `reconcile({ key: "id" })`, which preserves item references for unchanged IDs — so `<Index>` saw stale items when only a todo's `status` or `content` changed. Switching to `<For>` (which diffs by value reference, like the working TUI sidebar at `packages/tui/src/feature-plugins/sidebar/todo.tsx`) makes the dock reactive to in-place updates.

Single-file fix in `packages/app/src/pages/session/composer/session-todo-dock.tsx`. TUI was unaffected — it already uses `<For>`.
